```ADD_PAPER
1906.07832v3
This paper investigates the use of determinantal point processes (DPPs) within the kernel quadrature framework to achieve significantly tighter error bounds for numerical integration. By leveraging a truncated and saturated version of the RKHS kernel and the repulsive properties of DPPs, the authors derive error estimates that reflect the spectrum of the kernel, thereby delivering experimentally validated faster convergence rates than classic methods like Bayesian quadrature or herding. The extensive experimental analysis underscores the method’s potential for robust error control, offering a promising algorithmic avenue to combine DPP-based sampling with conformal prediction. This integration could be pivotal in enhancing the reliability and tightness of data-conditional, distribution-free risk guarantees by ensuring that uncertainty quantification is both robust and adaptively precise.
``` 
