Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Maximizing Data-Conditional, Distribution-Free Risk Guarantees via Bayesian Quadrature in Conformal Prediction

Overview:
We propose a simple yet innovative experimental framework that extends “Conformal Prediction as Bayesian Quadrature” by implementing three decision rules—Split Conformal Prediction (SCP), Conformal Risk Control (CRC), and a novel Bayesian quadrature-based Highest Posterior Density (HPD) rule using the Dirichlet quantile-spacing bound. Our experiments will target losses bounded by B=1 (e.g., miscoverage or false negative rate) and aim to achieve a per-trial failure probability (true risk exceeding α) of at most β (e.g., β = 0.95). We will validate on three suites: synthetic binomial loss, synthetic heteroskedastic regression loss (mimicking miscoverage), and multilabel classification on MS-COCO (controlling FNR).

Experimental Setup:
1. Common Framework:
   • For a candidate tuning parameter λ, compute individual losses ℓi(λ) on the calibration set.
   • Sort these losses to obtain ℓ(i) (with ℓ(n+1)=B).
   • Sample a vector U ~ Dirichlet(1,…,1) to compute L⁺ = Σᵢ Ui ℓ(i).
   • For a given λ, evaluate Pr(L⁺ ≤ α) through Monte Carlo simulation of the Dirichlet draws.
   • Choose the minimal λ such that Pr(L⁺ ≤ α) ≥ β.
   • Validate that CRC is recovered by observing that using E(L⁺) = (Σℓi + B)/(n+1) leads to the standard CRC decision and that for miscoverage loss, SCP corresponds to the (s)-order statistic selection.

2. Experiment 1 – Synthetic Binomial Loss:
   • Data Generation: Create a synthetic dataset where each instance’s “loss” follows a binomial-like model with a known ground truth risk (1 – λ). Control sample size n and a parameter K.
   • Implementation: For each calibration split, compute ℓi(λ). Run the Dirichlet simulation to assess the posterior probability constraint. Evaluate per-trial failure rate and prediction set “size” (or equivalent utility metric).
   • Ablations: Vary n, α, β, and K. Also analyze decision conservativeness by comparing β ∈ {0.80, 0.90, 0.95, 0.99}.

3. Experiment 2 – Synthetic Heteroskedastic Regression:
   • Data Generation: Simulate regression data with heteroskedastic noise such that miscoverage (with respect to regression prediction intervals) is the loss.
   • Implementation: Compute miscoverage losses ℓi(λ) for calibration splits. Use the Bayesian quadrature framework to compute L⁺ and select the minimal λ meeting Pr(L⁺ ≤ α) ≥ β.
   • Evaluation: Compare utility metrics (length of prediction intervals) alongside the per-trial failure rate.

4. Experiment 3 – MS-COCO Multilabel Classification:
   • Data: Use the MS-COCO dataset (with appropriate preprocessing using PyTorch/torchvision). Focus on controlling False Negative Rate (FNR) in multilabel prediction.
   • Model: Use an off-the-shelf multilabel classifier. Apply conformal methods to produce prediction sets that are adaptive in size and control misclassification risk.
   • Implementation: For a set of candidate λ values, compute instance-wise losses related to FNR. Run the Dirichlet-based Bayesian quadrature procedure to determine the optimal λ.
   • Evaluation: Measure failure rate in terms of FNR exceeding α and report utility via average prediction set size.

Technical Implementation & Reproducibility:
• Environment: Use Python ≥3.10 with numpy, scipy for Dirichlet simulations, and PyTorch/torchvision for the MS-COCO experiments.
• Fixed Seeds: Ensure computational reproducibility with fixed random seeds for synthetic data generation and model initialization.
• Deterministic Data Splits: Implement deterministic splitting of data (both synthetic and MS-COCO) using standard library functions and documented random state management.
• Code Modularization: Write modular code to easily swap between decision rules (SCP, CRC, Bayesian HPD) and to conduct ablation studies.

Conclusion:
This plan provides a clear, step-by-step experimental framework that integrates Bayesian quadrature within conformal prediction to yield tight, data-conditional risk guarantees. The multi-pronged evaluation—covering synthetic controlled settings and real-world multilabel classification—will demonstrate the reliability and utility of the proposed method. Ablation studies will further shed light on sensitivity to parameters (n, α, β, K) and decision aggressiveness, offering insights into practical deployment.

Next Steps:
- Begin with implementing the synthetic experiments and validating the Dirichlet-based evaluation mechanism.
- Extend the implementation to incorporate the MS-COCO pipeline and assess performance in a multilabel FNR setting.
- Analyze and document results comparing the three decision rules, ensuring that baselines (SCP and CRC) are consistently recovered under appropriate conditions.
  
End of Plan.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
