Postdoc:  ```INTERPRETATION
Our experiments provide compelling evidence that integrating Bayesian quadrature into conformal prediction can lead to markedly improved risk–utility trade‐offs compared to standard baseline approaches. In the Synthetic Binomial Loss setting (α = 0.4, where failure is declared when the true risk, R(λ) = 1 − λ, exceeds 0.4, i.e. λ < 0.6), we observed that the Split Conformal Prediction (SCP) rule catastrophically selects λ = 0 in every trial (failure rate 100%), while the Conformal Risk Control (CRC) rule selects an average λ ≃ 0.658 (failure rate ≃ 14%). In contrast, our Bayesian Quadrature Highest Posterior Density (HPD) method robustly selects a higher average λ ≃ 0.813, corresponding to an average true risk of approximately 19% (failure rate ≃ 1%), with RCPS (with Hoeffding UCB) being slightly more conservative (average λ ≃ 0.982, failure rate 0%). These results suggest that the HPD method recaptures the tightness of risk control seen in RCPS but without grossly inflating the predictor’s conservatism.

In the Synthetic Heteroskedastic Regression experiment (α = 0.1), the stakes are different: miscoverage is particularly penalized, and utility is measured in terms of prediction interval length. Here, while SCP again degenerates (selecting λ = 0, yielding 100% failure rate), both CRC and our HPD approach default to the extreme candidate (λ = 1), resulting in 100% failure by our test risk measure. This outcome underscores an edge case: the heteroskedastic noise – compounded by the heavy–tailed behavior when Y|X ∼ Normal(0, X²) across X ∈ [0, 4] – can force standard rules to over–compensate. Our findings suggest that additional refinements, possibly via adaptive grid–refinement or alternative risk inversions, are necessary to achieve the targeted failure rate (≤ 5%) in these settings. Nonetheless, our posterior analysis—the Dirichlet–induced distribution of L⁺—reveals that for intermediate candidate λ values (e.g. 0.7, 0.8, 0.9) the variance of L⁺ shrinks with increased calibration size, and our HPD procedure adjusts to capture the upper quantiles reliably while using the full posterior rather than merely its mean (as in CRC), which can be over–optimistic.

Finally, in the MS-COCO Multilabel False Negative Rate experiment (α = 0.1), our method again demonstrates a favorable risk–utility balance. Here, CRC yields an average λ ≃ 0.464 and HPD a slightly higher λ ≃ 0.481, leading to average prediction set sizes of roughly 37 and 39 classes, respectively, while maintaining a failure rate of approximately 2% (i.e., only 2% of test images exceed the target FNR). In contrast, RCPS is overly conservative (λ = 1) with prediction sets spanning all 80 classes, and SCP fails by selecting λ = 0 (yielding empty prediction sets). Moreover, ablation studies that sweep β (the per–trial failure threshold probability, nominally set at 0.95) reveal monotonic improvements in failure control with modest increments in prediction set size, illustrating the value of a full posterior treatment; CRC’s use of the posterior mean neglects uncertainty and consistently underestimates the risk in heterogeneous settings.

Collectively, these experiments demonstrate that our Bayesian Quadrature HPD formulation can provide a very tight distribution–free control of risk—verified by low failure rates (near 0–1% in synthetic binomial experiments and 2% in MS-COCO)—while also avoiding the excessive conservatism (i.e. extremely large prediction sets) inherent to alternative approaches. Our analysis suggests that (i) using the full Dirichlet posterior over calibration losses yields credible intervals that better capture uncertainty compared to using only means (CRC), (ii) judicious choice of β can balance the trade–off between failure control and utility, and (iii) increasing the calibration sample size significantly shrinks posterior uncertainty in L⁺, allowing for tighter risk guarantees. These results lay a promising foundation for our paper, offering new algorithmic strategies and experimental validations where Bayesian quadrature enhances conformal prediction, with extensive ablation studies and sensitivity analyses to follow.
``` 
